WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.748
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.748 --> 00:00:05.590
I help some foodies bring
home the bacon by profiling

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:06.670
their startup time.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.380
Can their architecture
stand the heat?

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.440
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.120
I gave a talk in
New York last week,

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.250
where I met the people
behind FoodEpix,

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.200
a social network
that only allows

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.060
you to share pictures of food--

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.490
no text, no audio, no emoji.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.980
Just a picture of food
and the geoloc tag

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.420
where it was taken at.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.267
Turns out their team had read my
previous articles on App Engine

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:30.850
startup performance and wanted
to reach out and see if I could

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:32.530
help them with a similar issue.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:36.010
While talking to their team
over a round of free pizza,

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.110
I got a clear sense
of their architecture.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.300
As a user uploaded
a picture of food,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.370
it was sent to a Compute
Engine instance, where

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:45.070
it ran some logic and
processing on the picture

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.260
to make sure it's
really an image of food.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.695
Now, I didn't really
figure anything was wrong

00:00:49.695 --> 00:00:51.820
until they showed me a
graph which looked something

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:52.810
like this.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:55.810
They were seeing about
300-second cold boot

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:57.910
time for their applications
while the response

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:01.364
latency for a request was in
the 300-millisecond range.

00:01:01.364 --> 00:01:03.280
Now, to be fair, by this
point I hadn't really

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.490
dug in to Compute Engine cold
boot performance that much.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:10.030
So I was happy to use this as an
excuse to investigate further.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:13.330
Now you may see a common trend
in my performance debugging

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.500
pattern at this point.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.020
When trying to track down
performance issues like this,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.050
I first like to remove my
code and establish a baseline

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.750
to know how badly my
code is impacting things.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.210
As such, the first steps
in figuring out the problem

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.970
was to run a quick test to
see if, number one, given

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.390
a static configuration, how long
Compute Engine instance takes

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.360
to boot up, and number
two, given changes

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.860
in that configuration,
how does that

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.610
impact the previous number?

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.400
So, let's start
with a simple test--

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.790
just 15 iterations of starting
an instance and timing how

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.370
long until we can SSH into it.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:50.170
On average, a two-CPU, 8-gig
VM took about 60 seconds

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.020
to boot up, which I think does
a nice job of establishing

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:53.980
our baseline.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.470
Now, I would assume that
improving resources should

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.210
see an improvement
in cold boot time

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:01.000
since there's more cycles
available to help the image get

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:01.910
up and running.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:04.300
So I doubled the
core and memory count

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.120
and saw an average
of about 63 seconds.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.009
A little worse, but still
within margin of error.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.760
This made me wonder if
increasing the CPU and RAM

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.640
caused boot time to
degrade linearly.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.920
To test that, I
increased both by 8x

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.550
and saw that the average
time was about 62 seconds.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.040
Which doesn't exactly
conform to my thought

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.540
that VM startup time would be
linear with resource requested.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.835
More so that these
timing fluctuations

00:02:30.835 --> 00:02:33.760
had something to do with the
small size of my sample set.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.280
That being said,
GCE did recently

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.630
update the max number of CPUs
you can request in a VM to 64.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:42.580
And when testing, we
did see that these

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.380
had consistently
higher cold boot

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:47.480
time than their less than
32 core counterparts.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.750
So, just to make sure we
wrap this all together,

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:53.560
let's do 200 configurations
of random CPU and RAM sizes

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.030
and see what we get.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:56.740
After testing this
about 20 times,

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.500
the average still looked
to be about 62 seconds.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.750
And to be clear, those
valleys and spikes

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.360
don't correlate with a
specific configuration.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.740
They move around
pretty regularly

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.520
between iterations of the test.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.420
This whole thing let me
know two important facts.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:13.510
Number one, cold boot
time of Compute Engine VMs

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:17.110
are not correlated with CPU/RAM
configurations directly.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:20.050
And number two, the
300-second cold boot time

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.880
that the FoodEpix
VMs were seeing

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:23.930
is being caused
by something else.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.200
So, let's get some more
information about bootup.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.790
Now, obviously,
we need to install

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.280
the operating system on the VM.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.040
Maybe we were using some
weird configuration of image

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:34.300
that I wasn't testing with.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.340
So, let's put them head to head.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.370
Keep the configuration the
same, but test a Linux instance

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:41.230
versus a Windows
instance and see

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:42.730
if that could be the problem.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:46.340
Not surprisingly, there was
some variation in the results.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:49.330
Most of the Linux-based
builds acted pretty similar.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:52.450
But the Windows builds
were drastically slower.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.700
And, just to get
visibility, I also

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.930
tested booting
clusters of machines.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:00.790
So seeing how one VM booted
versus 20 at the same time

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.820
showed some big differences
with the Windows VMs.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.460
But FoodEpix wasn't
using Windows VMs.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.740
They were using Linux
VMs, and only launching

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.100
about one or two at a time.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.550
So the choice of an image
isn't having anything

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:15.370
to do with their slow
cold boot performance.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:17.860
Now, one nice side effect
of profiling things

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.140
is that you typically
end up learning more

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.200
about some part of the system
that you didn't have visibility

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:23.920
to before.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.260
Or, rather, you end
up adding transparency

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.360
to a previous black box system.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.280
For me, running these
tests showed off

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.560
a few parts of the system
I didn't know about.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.470
Namely, that Compute Engine
has a distinct latency

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.320
in various stages of
VM bootup process.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:41.440
As a request comes in to
create a new instance,

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.130
GCE will move through the stages
with variants' latency attached

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:45.780
to it.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:47.530
Just to kick the tire,
I enlisted the help

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:49.660
of fellow Cloud DA Terry Ryan.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:51.820
And I was able to
get a profile on each

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:54.130
of these stages,
which actually didn't

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:55.600
reveal any specific problems.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.640
Other than the specific
startup process,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.650
you can expect about
seven seconds dedicated

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:02.480
to responding to a request
and about 18 seconds dedicated

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.520
to provisioning the VM
before any of your code

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:05.420
starts running.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:07.760
Now, at this point,
it's unclear what's

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.580
causing FoodEpix start
time to be so slow.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.000
But, we have eliminated CPU/RAM
configurations and image

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.650
selection as culprits.

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:17.450
Sadly, though, I only
have so much time

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:18.840
in each one of these videos.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.281
So if you want to see
what solved the problem,

00:05:21.281 --> 00:05:23.780
check out the rest of the "Cloud
Performance Atlas" content.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.730
And remember, when it
comes to performance,

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:26.810
every millisecond counts.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

